---
import { demoGate, demoManifest, honestUpgradesObserved, precheck, refusalCodes, rules, skillFiles } from "../../../data/docs.ts";
import type { CodeTab } from "../../ui/code-types.ts";
import Card from "../../ui/Card.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../ui/CodeBlock.astro";

/**
 * Write an upgrade that passes. Every number is read from `rules/demo/manifest.json` at build time
 * (see data/docs.ts); the rule names and reason texts come from lib/reasons.ts, and the attacks each
 * rule refuses from deployments/demo-gallery.json.
 */
const m = demoManifest;
const f = m.facts;
const lineage = m.lineage.join(" \u2192 ");
const refusals = skillFiles.find((file) => file.path === "references/refusals.md");
if (refusals === undefined) throw new Error("docs: data/docs.ts lists no references/refusals.md skill file");

const localChecks: CodeTab[] = [
  {
    id: "local",
    label: "Local checks",
    lines: [
      { note: "Linux or WSL, repository root, pinned Rust and Foundry." },
      { cmd: "forge build --root contracts" },
      { cmd: "constitution rules hash rules/demo/manifest.json" },
      { note: "Existing tests exercise the supplied implementations, not a contract you just wrote." },
      { cmd: "forge test --root contracts --match-contract VaultTest" },
      { cmd: "forge test --root contracts --match-contract VaultV3Test" },
    ],
  },
];
---

<div class="docs-prose">
  <p>
    The demo gate protects one vault proxy, so your candidate has to be a Vault variant: it keeps
    VaultV1's storage layout and its deposit and withdraw interface. The verifier compiles your
    source, then runs the rules below in order. A candidate is certified only if every one of them
    passes.
  </p>

  <h3>What the verifier does with your source</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Compile.</strong> With the pinned toolchain: solc <code>{f.solc}</code>, EVM {" "}
      <code>{f.evm_version}</code>. The runtime code hash must equal the one in your proposal.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Pre-check, called rule 0.</strong> The candidate is refused for any of these reasons:
      <ul>
        {precheck.map((text) => <li>{text}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>R1 to R5,</strong> described below.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>A private AI review,</strong> which can only refuse. It runs after R1 to R5 pass.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A certificate,</strong> valid for at most {f.max_cert_ttl_s} seconds. The gate then
      asks the NEAR MPC network to sign the exact upgrade, and anyone can relay it.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p class="muted">
    The pre-check recognises {lineage} as the current implementation, so an attack can be checked
    after an honest upgrade.
  </p>

  <h3>The five rules</h3>
  <p>
    Each rule shows what it checks, what your code needs, the numbers the demo gate committed, what
    the rule does not prove, and the attack on the <a href="/app">live demo</a> it refuses.
  </p>
  <ol class="docs-rules">
    {
      rules.map((rule) => (
        <Card as="li">
          <article class="docs-rule" aria-labelledby={`rule-${rule.id}`}>
            <h4 class="docs-rule__title" id={`rule-${rule.id}`}>
              <span class="docs-rule__id mono">{rule.id}</span>
              <span>{rule.name}</span>
            </h4>
            <div class="docs-field">
              <p class="docs-label">What it checks</p>
              <p>{rule.checks}</p>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-field">
              <p class="docs-label">What your code needs</p>
              <p>{rule.yourCode}</p>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-field">
              <p class="docs-label">Committed numbers</p>
              <dl class="docs-facts">
                {
                  rule.numbers.map((entry) => (
                    <div>
                      <dt>{entry.term}</dt>
                      <dd>{entry.value}</dd>
                    </div>
                  ))
                }
              </dl>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-field">
              <p class="docs-label">What it does not prove</p>
              <p>{rule.limit}</p>
            </div>
            {
              rule.refuses === null ? (
                <p class="docs-note docs-note--plain">
                  <strong>On the live demo:</strong> no gallery attack targets {rule.id}. It is
                  the rule an honest upgrade has to survive.
                </p>
              ) : (
                <p class="docs-note docs-note--refuse">
                  <strong>Refused on the live demo:</strong>{" "}
                  <a href="/app#attacks">{rule.refuses.title}</a>, reason {rule.refuses.reason}. {" "}
                  {rule.refuses.reasonText !== null && <> {rule.refuses.reasonText}</>}
                </p>
              )
            }
          </article>
        </Card>
      ))
    }
  </ol>

  <h3>A checklist before you propose</h3>
  <p>
    The five rules, as an authoring routine. The <a href="#skill">AI skill</a> carries the same
    list with worked honest and violating examples.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Use a new source path.</strong> Observe the current implementation and the committed
      rule set first. Do not edit the pinned VaultV1, MockToken or honest reference sources: that
      fails the pre-check, not a rule.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>R1.</strong> Preserve existing storage and append only. Keep the field order, types
      and namespace, and disable implementation initializers in the constructor as the honest
      examples do.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>R2.</strong> Keep total deposits at or below the vault's token balance, and keep the
      implementation slot, along bounded deposit and withdraw sequences. Debit only the withdrawing
      user, and reduce the aggregate before you transfer.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>R3.</strong> Remove the forbidden opcodes and every proxy-slot and cheatcode literal.
      Hiding such a constant behind a computation is not a fix.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>R4.</strong> Use an empty initializer, or <code>initializeV2(uint256)</code> with one
      32-byte word no larger than 2<sup>128</sup> &minus; 1. The honest V1 to V2 upgrade uses the cap {" "}
      <strong>1000</strong>, built with <code>cast calldata 'initializeV2(uint256)' 1000</code>. The
      honest V2 to V3 upgrade uses an empty <code>0x</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>R5.</strong> Preserve the committed corpus's exact behavior, state and logs, then pass
      the completed AI review. The AI can only veto: it cannot turn a failure into a pass. A cap so
      low that the replayed deposit reverts still fails R5, because R4 accepting it is not behavioral
      compatibility.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Test your own contract.</strong> Add tests for it, using the executor and proxy
      pattern in <code>contracts/test/VaultV3.t.sol</code>, before you deploy anything.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Then the private review</h3>
  <p>
    {f.ai.required ? "The gate requires" : "The gate allows"} a private review by {" "}
    <code>{f.ai.model}</code> on NEAR AI Cloud, with a verified <code>{f.ai.signature_kind}</code> {" "}
    response. It may propose up to {f.ai.max_candidates} counterexamples within {f.ai.timeout_s} seconds.
    The verifier replays each one deterministically, and a reproduced counterexample is a
    refusal. The model's word alone can never approve an upgrade. It is nondeterministic, so no
    find rate is promised.
  </p>

  <h3>Check locally first</h3>
  <p>
    Compile with the pinned toolchain and run the repository's tests. These commands are instructions,
    not a recorded pass, and a green preset test does not cover a new contract: add tests that seed
    balances, run your exact initializer, compare storage, returns and logs, and exercise withdrawals,
    an overdraw and a repeated initialization. Never deploy a candidate only because the unchanged
    preset tests pass.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock id="docs-local" label="Local checks" tabs={localChecks} title="repository root" />
  <p class="muted">
    The deterministic end-to-end check of the supplied lineage needs Docker, a NEAR sandbox and the
    pinned tool image, and it runs with the AI review deliberately unavailable, so it issues no
    certificate. There is no generic <code>check</code> command for a new candidate. Deploying it,
    and proposing it, each need explicit authorization.
  </p>

  <h3>When your proposal is refused</h3>
  <p>
    Read the stage detail and the accepted certificate, not only the headline: the first failure wins,
    and later stages can be <em>not run</em>, so a candidate that also breaks storage can stop at R1.
    These are the numeric pairs this page knows. The {" "}
    <a href={refusals.href}>refusal reference</a> adds every gate error prefix and every operational
    state, each with its recovery.
  </p>
  <dl class="docs-codes">
    {
      refusalCodes.map((entry) => (
        <div>
          <dt>
            <span class="docs-codes__code">{entry.code}</span>
            <span>{entry.rule}</span>
          </dt>
          <dd>{entry.text}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A refusal about your code</strong> needs a fix, a new deployment, a new source
      commitment and a new proposal. Never overwrite history.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A recoverable service failure</strong> normally leaves the same proposal Pending. Retry
      it with <code>verifier run</code>, with the original source, after any Retry-After wait.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>An incomplete required AI review</strong> is neither a rejection nor an approval. No
      certificate is issued and the proposal stays Pending.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>A realistic starting point</h3>
  <p>
    The honest examples live in <code>contracts/src/demo/</code>, and the manifest pins them by
    source hash. {" "}
    {
      honestUpgradesObserved
        ? " The gallery records an observed run for each honest upgrade."
        : " The gallery does not record an observed run for every honest upgrade, so treat them as references rather than proof that the demo gate has executed them."
    } {" "}
    Then propose your own with the <a href="#commands">headless commands</a>, or from the {" "}
    <a href="#tui">terminal UI</a>. The demo gate is {" "}
    <span class="mono">{demoGate.account}</span>.
  </p>
</div>
